  CLAUSE 4 -- does bw2 cross with the corrected denominator?
    bw2, audible jitter   d'  1.016 +- 0.040   against the bar 1.0
    pre-registered rule   clears only if the LOWER bound exceeds the
                          bar;  1.016 - 2.447 x 0.040 =  0.918
    -> *** STILL UNRESOLVED -- straddles the bar, count stays THREE
    and the ungated arm   d'  0.990 +- 0.039  -> UNRESOLVED
    SO THE CORRECTION DOES NOT CHANGE THE VERDICT.
    the same at matched level        dd'^2  -0.204 +-  0.038   -> SURVIVES: not the level

  CLAUSE 2 -- bw2's OWN TAX, and whether bw1/bw3 are dead weight. The
  cost is measured with f3 ALREADY SHIPPED, because that is the creature a
  four-axis code describes.
    bandwidths on, given f3, on F1   dd'^2   +7.605 +-  0.451  (+16.8 SE)
    of which freezing bw1+bw3 gives back     +0.266 +-  0.045
    the same at matched level        dd'^2   +4.882 +-  0.413  (+11.8 SE)
    and across the whole code, at matched level:
      f0    d'  4.318 ->  5.465   dd'^2  +11.219 +-  0.365   -> HELPED
      f1    d'  7.189 ->  7.521   dd'^2   +4.882 +-  0.413   -> HELPED
      f2    d'  8.308 ->  9.601   dd'^2  +23.150 +-  0.593   -> HELPED
      f3    d'  4.299 ->  5.431   dd'^2  +11.016 +-  0.269   -> HELPED
      bw1   d'  0.139 ->  0.844   dd'^2   +0.694 +-  0.055   -> the dose arriving, not a benefit
      bw2   d'  0.000 ->  0.970   dd'^2   +0.971 +-  0.064   -> the dose arriving, not a benefit
      bw3   d'  0.056 ->  0.744   dd'^2   +0.551 +-  0.033   -> the dose arriving, not a benefit
      -> 4 of the 4 formant axes (f0,f1,f2,f3) are HELPED at matched level

  CLAUSE 3 -- STATIC OR JITTER, for the bandwidth GROUP. With all three
  bandwidths held at rest, bw_gain still widens every resonator from 90 to
  ~149 Hz, so this contrast is the static widening with no added audible
  jitter. The single gain cannot split the static part per bandwidth.
    static widening alone, on F1     dd'^2   +8.085 +-  0.497
    the three bandwidths' jitter     dd'^2   -0.480 +-  0.069   -> a real cost

  CLAUSE 4 -- THE ORDERING, and this is the control that can fail. In the
  four-axis cell, freezing f3 (d' ~4.5, sd 47.6 Hz) must help F1 by MORE
  than freezing bw1+bw3 (d' ~0.9/0.8, sd 5.3/5.6 Hz), because jitter
  inflation scales with how audible the frozen axis's unsteadiness is. A
  reversal contradicts the account with every sign correct.
    freezing f3 on F1        dd'^2   +6.154 +-  0.209
    freezing bw1+bw3 on F1   dd'^2   +0.266 +-  0.045
    f3 minus bw1+bw3         dd'^2   +5.888 +-  0.197   -> ORDERED as predicted

  THE `all off-axis` ARM, AND WHY NOTHING MAY BE READ FROM IT. With only
  the target axis jittering, the within-class noise is RANK ONE and
  collinear with the signal, so this diagonal Mahalanobis counts the same
  ratio once per coefficient and returns dims*(reach/sd)^2. That ceiling is
  computed below from the reach and sd alone -- no ear involved.
    f0    measured    65.717   dims*(reach/sd)^2   357.103   0.184 of it
    f1    measured   317.803   dims*(reach/sd)^2   353.953   0.898 of it
    f2    measured   175.273   dims*(reach/sd)^2   324.469   0.540 of it
    f3    measured   196.248   dims*(reach/sd)^2   343.886   0.571 of it
    bw1   measured   409.029   dims*(reach/sd)^2   419.274   0.976 of it   <- SATURATED
    bw2   measured   347.260   dims*(reach/sd)^2   347.280   1.000 of it   <- SATURATED
    bw3   measured   392.900   dims*(reach/sd)^2   384.478   1.022 of it   <- SATURATED
    -> 3 of 7 axes sit within 5% of an arithmetic ceiling that has no ear
       in it. The column is WITHDRAWN, and so is run 2c's reading that its
       DIRECTION was informative: a saturated estimator has no direction.
       The off-axis burden is still real, but only arms where TWO OR MORE
       axes still jitter can measure it -- run 2d's f2-only freeze is one.

  THE FOURTH AXIS IS UNRESOLVED, AND THE BAR IS INSIDE THE ERROR. bw2
  reads d' 0.990 +- 0.039 with f3 shipped, which straddles the bar of 1.0.
  More reps would decide it -- the quantity is 10% of the way to
  resolvable at this n -- but the honest reading is that a 4th axis within
  noise of the audibility threshold cannot carry a vocabulary claim, and
  the usable count remains THREE.

  AND THE DROP IS NOT THE LEVEL: it is still -0.204 +- 0.038 when the two
  cells are rendered at matched output level, so the third formant costs
  bw2 through its jitter or its spectrum and not through its loudness.
  Renormalising the renderer would not give bw2 back.

  AND WHAT THE BANDWIDTHS COST, which is a separate question from whether
  bw2 is audible, and is answered either way:
  NOT A COST AT ALL -- A BENEFIT, AND IT SURVIVES THE LEVEL CONTROL. With
  f3 already shipped, turning the bandwidths on makes F1 MORE separable by
  +7.605 +- 0.451 (+16.8 SE), and +4.882 +- 0.413 of it is still there when the
  two cells are rendered at MATCHED OUTPUT LEVEL. axisear's -3.211 describes
  a creature WITHOUT a third formant; in the creature a four-axis code
  actually describes, the bandwidth gain PAYS.

  AND THE MECHANISM IS THE STATIC WIDENING (+8.085 +- 0.497), not the jitter
  (-0.480 +- 0.069). Widening every resonator from 90 to ~149 Hz spreads each
  formant's energy over more mel channels, so the same formant shift moves
  MORE cepstral coefficients. The three bandwidths' own unsteadiness is a
  separate and much smaller term.

  SO `render_bw_gain = 1` IS A CANDIDATE GENOME CHANGE ON ITS OWN MERITS,
  independent of whether bw2 is usable as an axis -- it would improve the
  axes the creature ALREADY has. It is not free to adopt: it moves the
  determinism hash and voids every cross-genome absolute
  (`aibaby-vocal-calibration-invariant`), and `kVLScoreFormant` would have
  to show the gain in a naming task before any capability is claimed.

  AND CLAUSE 4 HELD: freezing f3 beats freezing bw1+bw3 by +5.888 +- 0.197,
  the ordering jitter inflation requires. The control could have failed
  with every sign correct, and did not.
